{
  "criteria": {
    "H1": "Regression EAC (with change-order exposure) reduces mean absolute percentage error by at least 20% versus the CPI-based EAC at every checkpoint of 30%, 40%, 50%, 60% and 70% complete, with Holm-adjusted paired Wilcoxon p < 0.05 at each of those five checkpoints.",
    "H2": "In high-change-volume projects, adding pending change-order exposure to the regression reduces per-project mean absolute percentage error (averaged over the 30-70% checkpoints) by at least 10% with a bootstrap 95% interval above zero and Holm-adjusted p < 0.05 (Holm across the three volumes).",
    "H3": "The earned-schedule finish forecast has a lower mean absolute finish-date error than the traditional SPI(cost) forecast at every checkpoint of 50%, 60%, 70%, 80% and 90% complete, with Holm-adjusted paired Wilcoxon p < 0.05 at each of those five checkpoints.",
    "H4": "With reporting delayed by 4 weeks, the mean absolute percentage error at the 50% checkpoint is at most 1.5 times the error with no delay, for both the CPI-based and the regression EAC (point estimates).",
    "note": "Thresholds (20%, 10%, 1.5 times) and the meaning of \"materially\" and \"gracefully\" are the author's operationalisations, fixed before the reported run. Smaller or larger effects are reported as measured."
  },
